{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013Z5C8oLxLWEL5ivSHSXVku","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Shared primary-reference packet\n2\t\n3\tIdentical in both arms. This packet supplies library/format semantics, not finding\n4\tlocations or an answer key. Recorded 2026-10-23. Model web lookup is unavailable.\n5\t\n6\t- Express 4 error handling: https://expressjs.com/en/4x/guide/error-handling/\n7\t  Official framework documentation, retrieved 2026-10-23. Rejected async-handler\n8\t  promises need explicit forwarding to the framework error path.\n9\t- Node 20 runtime options: https://nodejs.org/download/release/latest-v20.x/docs/api/cli.html#--unhandled-rejectionsmode\n10\t  Official runtime documentation, retrieved 2026-10-23. The default unhandled\n11\t  rejection mode raises an uncaught exception when no relevant hook is installed.\n12\t- Desktop manifest 0.1: https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/mcpb/blob/v0.1.0/MANIFEST.md#user-configuration\n13\t  Versioned format specification. The sensitive string option requests masked\n14\t  input and secure storage; its default is false. Installed-host behavior still\n15\t  needs evidence.\n16\t- MCP security guidance: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices\n17\t  Protocol-project guidance, bundled review date 2026-10-22. Scope credential,\n18\t  process and transport boundaries to the component actually supplied.\n19\t- TypeScript SDK redirect advisory: https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw\n20\t  Maintainer advisory, published 2026-10-02, checked 2026-10-23. SDK HTTP clients\n21\t  before 1.32.0 can forward custom headers and request bodies across redirects.\n22\t  Check package, version and use of the affected client; an unrelated component\n23\t  is not vulnerable merely because it participates in MCP.\n24\t- OWASP Logging: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html\n25\t  Guidance, bundled review date 2026-10-22. Keep credentials out of diagnostic\n26\t  output and distinguish diagnostic sinks from authorized secret consumers.\n27\t\n28\tThe external semantics above were rechecked on 2026-10-24 before the campaign.\n29\tSee verification-2026-10-24.json for short primary excerpts, the versioned manifest\n30\tblob ID and the maintainer advisory ranges/dates. These records are identical in\n31\tboth arms and contain no fixture labels or answer locations.\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02d1ced0-406a-4330-b848-b89e3456ecab","uuid":"cecab6f8-2b12-4cfa-8907-ec9a034e1a66","timestamp":"2026-10-24T20:18:05.227Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-review-s1ns512q/research/references.md","content":"# Shared primary-reference packet\n\nIdentical in both arms. This packet supplies library/format semantics, not finding\nlocations or an answer key. Recorded 2026-10-23. Model web lookup is unavailable.\n\n- Express 4 error handling: https://expressjs.com/en/4x/guide/error-handling/\n  Official framework documentation, retrieved 2026-10-23. Rejected async-handler\n  promises need explicit forwarding to the framework error path.\n- Node 20 runtime options: https://nodejs.org/download/release/latest-v20.x/docs/api/cli.html#--unhandled-rejectionsmode\n  Official runtime documentation, retrieved 2026-10-23. The default unhandled\n  rejection mode raises an uncaught exception when no relevant hook is installed.\n- Desktop manifest 0.1: https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/mcpb/blob/v0.1.0/MANIFEST.md#user-configuration\n  Versioned format specification. The sensitive string option requests masked\n  input and secure storage; its default is false. Installed-host behavior still\n  needs evidence.\n- MCP security guidance: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices\n  Protocol-project guidance, bundled review date 2026-10-22. Scope credential,\n  process and transport boundaries to the component actually supplied.\n- TypeScript SDK redirect advisory: https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk/security/advisories/GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw\n  Maintainer advisory, published 2026-10-02, checked 2026-10-23. SDK HTTP clients\n  before 1.32.0 can forward custom headers and request bodies across redirects.\n  Check package, version and use of the affected client; an unrelated component\n  is not vulnerable merely because it participates in MCP.\n- OWASP Logging: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html\n  Guidance, bundled review date 2026-10-22. Keep credentials out of diagnostic\n  output and distinguish diagnostic sinks from authorized secret consumers.\n\nThe external semantics above were rechecked on 2026-10-24 before the campaign.\nSee verification-2026-10-24.json for short primary excerpts, the versioned manifest\nblob ID and the maintainer advisory ranges/dates. These records are identical in\nboth arms and contain no fixture labels or answer locations.\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013Z5C8oLxLWEL5ivSHSXVku","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMgNdHcDsNGV5w2MwKQb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Review of `repo/service/routes.mjs`\n\nThe component is small, and I found three real issues. The first is the most serious.\n\n## 1. Unhandled promise rejection can crash the process (High, availability)\n`routes.mjs:16-25`: the `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp` handlers are `async` and `await session.handleRequest(req, res)` with no `try/catch`.\n- Express 4.22.2 doesn't forward rejected promises from async handlers to `next`. The Express docs in `research/` say this.\n- The app installs no `unhandledRejection` hook. Node 20's default mode (`throw`) turns the rejection into an uncaught exception, which terminates the process.\n- Any error thrown or rejected by `handleRequest` therefore kills the server for every session. A malformed request, a transport that has already closed, or a client that disconnects mid-stream could each trigger it. Only a holder of the gateway secret can reach these handlers, but a single trusted client can still take the service down.\n- Fix: wrap each handler in `try/catch`, calling `next(err)` or returning a 500 if headers haven't been sent. Alternatively, use a shared `wrap(fn)` helper.\n\n## 2. Sessions are never cleaned up (Medium, resource exhaustion)\n- `POST /mcp/init` (`:10-14`) adds a transport to `sessions` on every call and never removes it.\n- `DELETE /mcp` delegates to the transport but doesn't remove the map entry. Sessions also have no TTL and no cap.\n- Even for the single trusted principal, a buggy or looping client leaks memory and any resources held by `createTransport()`. A closed transport also stays reachable, which makes issue 1 more likely.\n- Fix: delete the entry after a successful `DELETE` and on transport close. Add an idle timeout and a maximum session count.\n\n## 3. Session lookup uses a plain object (Low–Medium, robustness)\n- `const sessions = {}` is looked up with a client-controlled header (`:17`, `:22`).\n- A header like `__proto__`, `constructor` or `toString` resolves to an inherited property, which is truthy. `session.handleRequest` is then not a function, and the resulting `TypeError` becomes the unhandled rejection from issue 1. That makes it a crash trigger that needs no valid session.\n- The header can also be an array or `undefined`.\n- Fix: use `new Map()` or `Object.create(null)`, and check that the header is a string.\n\n## Minor observations\n- **Secret comparison (`:7`):** `!==` on the Authorization header isn't constant-time. This is low risk for a high-entropy secret behind a gateway, but `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length buffers is cheap to add.\n- **No response on a failed init:** `createTransport()` at `:12` can throw synchronously. Express 4 does catch synchronous errors in non-async handlers, so this is fine as written, but the session ID shouldn't be registered until creation succeeds. It currently is registered after creation, so that ordering is correct.\n- **Session ID is not bound to anything:** this is acceptable per the README (one trusted principal, with IDs as correlation handles only). I didn't count it as a finding.\n- **No `POST /mcp` handler:** only `/mcp/init`, `GET` and `DELETE` are registered, so the transport can't receive client-to-server JSON-RPC messages over `POST`. If the host's `createTransport` expects that, it's a functional gap. I can't confirm it from this repo.\n\n## Checked and not an issue\n- **Auth gating:** `app.use('/mcp', …)` covers `/mcp/init`, `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp`, and it runs before dispatch. A missing `GATEWAY_KEY` fails closed at startup.\n- **`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` redirect advisory (GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw):** not applicable. The package isn't a dependency, and this component is a server, not an SDK HTTP client.\n- **Manifest `sensitive` option:** there's no manifest in this repo, so it doesn't apply.\n- **Logging:** the code writes no logs, so no credentials leak there.\n- **Out of scope per the README:** the `createTransport` implementation, the gateway and secret store, host audit, framing limits, ingress policy and process supervision. These need host evidence.\n\nI read the files only and ran nothing."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2627,"cache_read_input_tokens":6813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2627},"output_tokens":728,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02d1ced0-406a-4330-b848-b89e3456ecab","uuid":"c6eb5c50-7872-49f4-91ac-32f9f5d32b36","timestamp":"2026-10-24T20:18:16.919Z","request_id":"req_011CgMgNd1z8V7z789u6QMZJ"}
{"duration_api_ms":22700,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"02d1ced0-406a-4330-b848-b89e3456ecab","total_cost_usd":0.049044000000000004,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4854,"cache_read_input_tokens":21620,"output_tokens":2745,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":375},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4854,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1496,"cache_read_input_tokens":6813,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2627,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2627},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5-5":{"inputTokens":8,"outputTokens":2745,"cacheReadInputTokens":21620,"cacheCreationInputTokens":4854,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.049044000000000004,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":375,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":10,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"# Review of `repo/service/routes.mjs`\n\nThe component is small, and I found three real issues. The first is the most serious.\n\n## 1. Unhandled promise rejection can crash the process (High, availability)\n`routes.mjs:16-25`: the `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp` handlers are `async` and `await session.handleRequest(req, res)` with no `try/catch`.\n- Express 4.22.2 doesn't forward rejected promises from async handlers to `next`. The Express docs in `research/` say this.\n- The app installs no `unhandledRejection` hook. Node 20's default mode (`throw`) turns the rejection into an uncaught exception, which terminates the process.\n- Any error thrown or rejected by `handleRequest` therefore kills the server for every session. A malformed request, a transport that has already closed, or a client that disconnects mid-stream could each trigger it. Only a holder of the gateway secret can reach these handlers, but a single trusted client can still take the service down.\n- Fix: wrap each handler in `try/catch`, calling `next(err)` or returning a 500 if headers haven't been sent. Alternatively, use a shared `wrap(fn)` helper.\n\n## 2. Sessions are never cleaned up (Medium, resource exhaustion)\n- `POST /mcp/init` (`:10-14`) adds a transport to `sessions` on every call and never removes it.\n- `DELETE /mcp` delegates to the transport but doesn't remove the map entry. Sessions also have no TTL and no cap.\n- Even for the single trusted principal, a buggy or looping client leaks memory and any resources held by `createTransport()`. A closed transport also stays reachable, which makes issue 1 more likely.\n- Fix: delete the entry after a successful `DELETE` and on transport close. Add an idle timeout and a maximum session count.\n\n## 3. Session lookup uses a plain object (Low–Medium, robustness)\n- `const sessions = {}` is looked up with a client-controlled header (`:17`, `:22`).\n- A header like `__proto__`, `constructor` or `toString` resolves to an inherited property, which is truthy. `session.handleRequest` is then not a function, and the resulting `TypeError` becomes the unhandled rejection from issue 1. That makes it a crash trigger that needs no valid session.\n- The header can also be an array or `undefined`.\n- Fix: use `new Map()` or `Object.create(null)`, and check that the header is a string.\n\n## Minor observations\n- **Secret comparison (`:7`):** `!==` on the Authorization header isn't constant-time. This is low risk for a high-entropy secret behind a gateway, but `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length buffers is cheap to add.\n- **No response on a failed init:** `createTransport()` at `:12` can throw synchronously. Express 4 does catch synchronous errors in non-async handlers, so this is fine as written, but the session ID shouldn't be registered until creation succeeds. It currently is registered after creation, so that ordering is correct.\n- **Session ID is not bound to anything:** this is acceptable per the README (one trusted principal, with IDs as correlation handles only). I didn't count it as a finding.\n- **No `POST /mcp` handler:** only `/mcp/init`, `GET` and `DELETE` are registered, so the transport can't receive client-to-server JSON-RPC messages over `POST`. If the host's `createTransport` expects that, it's a functional gap. I can't confirm it from this repo.\n\n## Checked and not an issue\n- **Auth gating:** `app.use('/mcp', …)` covers `/mcp/init`, `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp`, and it runs before dispatch. A missing `GATEWAY_KEY` fails closed at startup.\n- **`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` redirect advisory (GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw):** not applicable. The package isn't a dependency, and this component is a server, not an SDK HTTP client.\n- **Manifest `sensitive` option:** there's no manifest in this repo, so it doesn't apply.\n- **Logging:** the code writes no logs, so no credentials leak there.\n- **Out of scope per the README:** the `createTransport` implementation, the gateway and secret store, host audit, framing limits, ingress policy and process supervision. These need host evidence.\n\nI read the files only and ran nothing.","ttft_ms":1494,"type":"result","duration_ms":22765,"uuid":"2dcbe17f-3637-4aa8-a7a2-8fd43a89ef71","ttft_stream_ms":705,"time_to_request_ms":34,"first_content_frame_ms":705,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"claude-sonne